{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>This month.</b> Rabbi Judah the Persian said that Israel counted according to solar years like the practice of the uncircumcised, and his proof is \"you shall keep this statute at its appointed season\" (Exodus 13:10), for a lunar year is not equal, since the days of plowing and harvest depend solely on the course of the sun according to its inclination northward or southward. But this is not correct; I shall now explain to you.\n\nWe know that the moon's circuit in its sphere at a defined point against the zodiac, until it returns to that same point, is approximately 27 days and one-third of a day. Now suppose it conjoined with the sun when it was at the first point at the beginning of Aries; upon its return to the head of Aries, it does not find the sun there, for the sun has already traveled in its mean course 27 degrees, since the motion of both in true motion is from west to east, opposite to the upper motion. The moon then traversed those degrees that the sun had already traversed, and the moon also traversed the portions that the sun traveled during the moon's traversal of the aforementioned degrees — two days and approximately 45 hours. They conjoined at the end of 29 and a half days, 2 halakhot of an hour, and 73 parts of the parts of Israel. And the year in truth belongs to the sun alone, for it produces the seasons of cold and heat, summer and winter, which are the four seasons of the year. Then we consider it was at the beginning of the head of Aries, and it travels in its sphere 365 days and approximately a quarter of a day until it returns a second time; because of this it is called a year. The moon has no year at all, just as the sun has no month at all, for nothing is renewed in the sun; rather, renewal belongs to the light of the moon. Because of this, a month is also called ירח, on account of the renewal of the moon's light, for it has no light in true motion except from the sun. Therefore the sun is eclipsed only at the moment of its conjunction with the moon, if it is at the head of Aries or at its tail, and the moon is eclipsed only when it is opposite the sun at night in the aforementioned positions.\n\nSince the year of the uncircumcised is close to the solar year, and they saw that the moon renews itself twelve times in the solar year, they divided the days of the year into twelve and called each division a month, because it approximates the days of lunar renewal. This agreement among them is not according to nature. The Ishmaelites too, because their festivals depend on the days of lunar months, begin the month from the night of the moon's sighting. They saw that there are 12 lunar months in a solar year and found no reckoning closer than this, for the difference between the two numbers is only 11 days; therefore they made all their years lunar, and each year their Passover shifts backward 11 days, until 33 solar years equal 34 lunar years, for these are not according to nature.\n\nHaShem commanded in His Torah to keep the festival at its appointed season, for He said, \"Keep the month of Aviv and make Passover to YHWH\" (Deuteronomy 16:1). If Aviv is not found in the middle of the month, Passover is made in the adjacent month. Thus He called the festival of Shavuot \"the festival of harvest\" (Exodus 23:16), and Sukkot, \"the festival of ingathering\" (ibid.), for harvest and ingathering depend on Aviv. We see that He said Passover may not be made except in the middle of a lunar month, and likewise Sukkot. He commanded to keep the days of the lunar month so that the festivals would be kept at their proper times.\n\nMoses did not mention in the Torah whether to count 12 months or 13; rather, he said: in the month in which Aviv is found, begin to count, and it is called first, whether the year has 12 months or 13. Thus it is written, \"in the second month\" (Numbers 10:11), \"in the third month\" (ibid. 19:1), and all follow this pattern.\n\nNisan, Iyyar, Sivan, and all the month-names are not sacred language but Chaldean; therefore you will not find a single one of them in Scripture — only in the prophecies of Zechariah, Daniel, Ezra, and the Scroll of Esther, who were in exile. This is the meaning of \"the first month,\" which is called Nisan in Chaldean. We found only in the sacred language that three months are called differently: ירח זיו (1 Kings 6:1), בירח האיתנים (ibid. 8:2), בירח בול (ibid. 6:38). If we pursue the plain sense, ירח זיו is not Nisan but Iyyar, for it is written \"the second month\" (1 Kings 6:1), which is known to be second to the first of Israel's months. The word למלך (\"of the reign of\") does not attach to \"the month\" but to \"in the fourth year of Solomon's reign\" (ibid.), for we do not find in the reckoning of any kings that the months are counted, only the year of his reign. The proof is what is written in Chronicles: \"in the second month, in the fourth year of his reign\" (2 Chronicles 3:2).\n\nOne may challenge Judah the Persian: all years are not equal, for variation comes from the configuration of the heavenly ministers — rains may increase or decrease, and heat or cold may increase or decrease in any given month beyond the known pattern. Therefore Aviv is not found when the sun returns to the point where it was at the beginning of the previous year when Aviv was found then, and between Aviv and Aviv there may be more than a full year plus additional weeks. Therefore Israel relied on the Aviv found in their land, in whatever month it was found, for the season of Aviv varies in all lands according to the distance of each land from the equator.\n\nNow I give you a principle: although Israel in the Temple looked to the Aviv, the intercalation of the year was not entrusted to them, but to the court, which also considered matters other than Aviv, as written in the Mishnah.\n\nMoses did not explain to us in the Torah how years and months are fixed, or what to do if Aviv is not found. This is a great wonder: how he explained the laws of all the afflictions of the leper — which concern a single individual and do not persist at all times — yet left the matter of the festivals, which all Israel is obligated to keep at all times, with the penalty of excision for eating leaven on Passover and for eating on Yom HaKippurim.\n\nIf he relied on the molad, he should have explained whether according to the mean course or according to the true molad, which is against the zodiac, for sometimes between the mean molad and the true molad there is a difference of nearly 14 hours, sometimes to add and sometimes to subtract. Because of this our holy ancestors said: sometimes it comes long, and sometimes it comes short. This cannot be known except by one skilled in the wisdom of the stars. He should have explained for which place on earth the molad is reckoned, for between Jerusalem and the city called Luqa there is more than a 3-hour difference. If he relied on sighting, he should have explained for which place the sighting applies, for the arc of visibility changes in every place according to the latitude, and according to the moon's distance from the ecliptic, with the appearance varying in longitude and latitude, altitude and lowness and atmosphere. These matters require lengthy explanation. If the atmosphere is not clear, Rosh Chodesh is made for two days out of doubt — and this is a sign that reliance is placed on the proclamation of the court in every generation.\n\nThere was a tradition in their hands that the court always fixes seven years of intercalation in every 19 years, which are 235 months — 6,939 days, plus two-thirds of a day, plus 1,095 parts. These are exactly 19 solar years without addition or subtraction. Do not be concerned about the hour and 483 parts regarding the tekufah of Samuel, for we know this was not hidden from him; rather, he took an approximate approach in his time for the people of his generation, for today the difference between the true tekufah and his tekufah is approximately 9 days. Do not wonder at this, for the baraita said it: one who wishes to know the position of the moon should reckon the years of the world and make them into months, and assign each month 29 and a half days and two halakhot of an hour, which are 793 parts. This is short by 73 parts, and so more than two-thirds of a day has accumulated from them in every cycle. The gap between the reckoning of moladot from the days of creation and our reckoning of the molad today is approximately half a year.\n\nIt is now clear to you that the totality of our years returns in every cycle to solar years, and our months are always lunar months. Blessed is HaShem who guided us in a straight path.\n\nI now give you a proof from Scripture that the festivals depend on the court, for it is written: \"And the king took counsel with Hezekiah to make Passover in the second month\" (2 Chronicles 30:2). If there were a tradition in their hands to look to the matter of Aviv, as those who dispute our rabbis of blessed memory say, why did he take counsel to fix Passover in the second month? If their words are true, Hezekiah did three evil things: first, that he did not offer Passover at its proper time though he was pure — and why did he delay it because of the multitude that had not come? This is not written in the Torah but only in the words of tradition. Second, that he ate and fed all Israel leaven in the first month. Third, that he fabricated from his own heart to make the festival of unleavened bread in the second month for seven days — and this may not be done, for HaShem did not command making the Passover festival in the second month, but only the eating of the Passover sacrifice on one night, for all who are impure eat unleavened bread in the first month with all Israel. Far be it, far be it that Hezekiah did such evils, for Scripture says of him: \"he did not turn aside from all the commandments of YHWH, right or left,\" and he himself said in his prayer: \"Remember now, I pray, how I walked before You and did what is good in Your eyes\" (2 Kings 20:3).\n\nNow I shall explain to you. Know that Hezekiah consulted with the entire assembly, at the entry of the first month, to make Passover in the month that follows it, because of the multitude that had not come and the priests who had not sanctified themselves sufficiently (2 Chronicles 30:19). He intercalated the year, and the Passover he made was a first Passover, performed at its proper time. The proof is that HaShem smote those who ate the Passover improperly (2 Chronicles 30:18) — and if it was not done at its proper time, why did HaShem smite them, since the Passover would not have been acceptable before Him? And if Hezekiah sinned, how did he pray on their behalf and HaShem hear his prayer and heal the people (ibid. 20:20)?\n\nIf one contends that the sages of blessed memory did not grant him permission to intercalate Nisan — the answer is true: they said that the practice of every court is to examine at the end of the month of Adar whether the year requires intercalation. Hezekiah did not do so, but it is a minor error to intercalate the year at Rosh Chodesh. Because the intercalated month always comes before the first month, it is therefore always the head of the months, and the festivals in the seventh month from it — this is the meaning of \"it is first for you, for the months of the year.\"\n\nWe know that many commandments in the Torah are a remembrance of the Exodus from Egypt, and this is fitting to be the beginning of Israel's reckoning. Thus it is written: \"And it came to pass in the eightieth year and four hundredth year after the Israelites came out of the land of Egypt, in the fourth year, in the month of Ziv, which is the second month\" (1 Kings 6:1). The nations say: if the year is built from the months, then the month of Aviv is the foundation and head; why then do you begin your count from the seventh month and call it Rosh HaShanah? The answer, our rabbis of blessed memory already mentioned: there are four new years. We see explicitly that the Jubilee year is sanctified \"in the seventh month, on the tenth of the month,\" and furthermore HaShem commanded to read the Torah at the appointed time of the sabbatical year, on the festival of Sukkot (Deuteronomy 31:10), \"so that they may hear and so that they may learn\" (ibid. 12). If the beginning of the sabbatical year were from Nisan, why did He not command to read the Torah at the festival of unleavened bread? Half a year would have stood idle. It is written, \"the festival of ingathering, at the turn of the year\" (Exodus 34:22), and in another place, \"the festival of ingathering, at the going out of the year\" (ibid. 23:16). We can further learn from the way of the Torah and from the way of nature, for it first says \"you shall not sow,\" and afterward \"you shall not reap\" (Leviticus 25:11), since sowing is adjacent to Tishrei, not Nisan. Furthermore, if we set the beginning of the sabbatical year at Nisan, one who sows in the sixth year will not reap in the seventh, for the harvest is after Nisan and the sabbatical year has already begun, nor may he sow in the sabbatical year. Thus the words of our sages of blessed memory are correct." "<b>Head of months.</b> Rabbi Moses the Priest said: one need not say ראש חדש but only חדש alone, for the meaning is the day of the renewal of the moon's light. Thus it is written חדש ושבת (Isaiah 1:13), חדשיכם ומועדיכם (ibid. 14), מידי חדש בחדשו (ibid. 66:23). Others challenged him: if so, why does it say בחדש השביעי באחד לחדש (Numbers 29:1)? He answered to remove the doubt. They also challenged him on ובראשי חדשיכם (Numbers 28:11), and he answered that it speaks of the month of Nisan in every year, for Nisan alone is the head of months. They asked him: if so, what is the offering of the remaining months? He answered that there it is written זאת עולת חדש בחדשו לחדשי השנה (ibid. 14), in the very same passage. Similarly he said: וביום שמחתכם ובמועדיכם ובראשי חדשיכם ותקעתם בחצצרת (Numbers 10:10), for it is the beginning of the year. But we will not abandon the words of our rabbis of blessed memory on account of our own reasonings, for they said that in every month they blow the trumpets, and this is correct — whether to say חדש or ראש חדש. And it is written עד חדש ימים (Numbers 11:20), whose meaning is that the days of his month should be fulfilled."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "Where Ibn Ezra cites 'יג י' as the prooftext for the uncircumcised nations' solar practice, I resolved this to Exodus 13:10 based on the verse's content ('at its appointed season') and canonical ordering. The text וזאתה appears to be a form of וזאת; translated as 'and this' without further expansion. The phrase שס״ה (365) is rendered numerically. The Luqa mentioned is identified by Ibn Ezra in other works as a location significant for astronomical observation. The verse reference in the challenge to Judah the Persian — 'if all years are not equal' — is left without added connective tissue as the argument stands compressed. Where Ibn Ezra writes 'the upper motion,' this refers to the diurnal motion of the primum mobile in medieval cosmology."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T20:25:36.754447807Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}